.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager (fedora-dwm build)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X. It manages windows in tiled, monocle
and floating layouts, plus several additional layouts (spiral, dwindle, deck,
bstack, grid, and more) added by the vanitygaps patch. Either layout can be
applied dynamically, optimising the environment for the application in use
and the task performed.
.P
In tiled layouts windows are managed in a master and stacking area. The master
area on the left contains one window by default, and the stacking area on the
right contains all other windows. The number of master area windows can be
adjusted from zero to an arbitrary number. In monocle layout all windows are
maximised to the screen size. In floating layout windows can be resized and
moved freely. Dialog windows are always managed floating, regardless of the
layout applied.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags. Layout,
master-area count, and master-area size are remembered per tag (pertag
patch).
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays all available tags, the
layout, the title of the focused window, and the text read from the root window
name property. Unlike stock dwm, the status text is drawn on every monitor,
not just the focused one (statusallmons patch), and supports inline color and
drawing escape codes (status2d patch, see
.B STATUS TEXT FORMATTING
below). A floating window is indicated with an empty square and a maximised
floating window is indicated with a filled square before the window's title.
The selected tags are indicated with a different color. The tags of the
focused window are indicated with a filled square in the top left corner. The
tags which are applied to one or more windows are indicated with an empty
square in the top left corner.
.P
dwm draws a border around windows to indicate the focus state; its width can
be adjusted live (setborderpx patch). Windows in the same stack area can be
given different relative sizes via cfact.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command, or by any status bar generator (this build installs
.BR slstatus (1)).
.TP
.B Button1
click on a tag label to display all windows with that tag; click on the layout
label cycles the layout.
.TP
.B Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes all windows with that tag to/from the view;
right-click on the layout label jumps to the spiral layout.
.TP
.B Super\-Button1
click on a tag label applies that tag to the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes that tag to/from the focused window.
.SS Keyboard commands
MOD refers to the Super (Windows) key; ALT refers to Alt. The full list below
is also kept as plain text in
.I keybinds.txt
in the repository root, symlinked to
.I ~/.config/dwm/keybinds.txt
by install.sh and viewable at any time with MOD+space.
.TP
.B MOD\-Return
Spawn a terminal (kitty).
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-Return
Open a file manager (thunar).
.TP
.B MOD\-d
Spawn
.BR dmenu (1)
for launching other programs.
.TP
.B MOD\-space
Show the keybind reference (keybinds.txt) in a terminal.
.TP
.B MOD\-s
Take a screenshot (flameshot).
.TP
.B MOD\-b
Launch a browser (brave).
.TP
.B MOD\-m
Launch a browser (mullvad-browser).
.TP
.B MOD\-t
Launch a GTK theme switcher (lxappearance).
.TP
.B MOD\-w / MOD\-Shift\-w
Next / previous wallpaper.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-Shift\-l
Lock the screen (slock).
.TP
.B MOD\-x
Open the power/logout menu.
.TP
.B MOD\-,
Focus previous monitor, if any.
.TP
.B MOD\-.
Focus next monitor, if any.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-,
Send focused window to previous monitor, if any.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-.
Send focused window to next monitor, if any.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-1
Set tile layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-2
Set floating layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-3
Set monocle layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-4
Set centered-master layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-5
Set bottom-stack layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-6
Set grid layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-7
Set spiral layout.
.TP
.B MOD\-j
Focus next window.
.TP
.B MOD\-k
Focus previous window.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-j
Move focused window down the stack, swapping it with the next client.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-k
Move focused window up the stack, swapping it with the previous client.
.TP
.B MOD\-i
Increase number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-d
Decrease number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B MOD\-l
Increase master area size.
.TP
.B MOD\-h
Decrease master area size.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-l
Grow focused window's share of its stack area (cfact).
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-h
Shrink focused window's share of its stack area (cfact).
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-o
Reset focused window's cfact to the default.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-u
Increase all gaps.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-Shift\-u
Decrease all gaps.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-g
Toggle gaps on/off.
.TP
.B MOD\-ALT\-Shift\-g
Reset gaps to the configured default.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-bracketleft
Decrease border width.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-bracketright
Increase border width.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-BackSpace
Reset border width to the configured default.
.TP
.B MOD\-q
Close focused window.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-f
Toggle real fullscreen for the focused window (as opposed to the bar-hiding
monocle-layout trick stock dwm uses).
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-space
Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-z
Un-float every visible floating window on the current tag and retile them.
.TP
.B MOD\-Tab
Toggles to the previously selected tag(s).
.TP
.B MOD\-Left / MOD\-Right
Cycle the view to the previous / next tag.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-[1..9]
Apply nth tag to focused window.
.TP
.B MOD\-Control\-Shift\-[1..9]
Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.
.TP
.B MOD\-[1..9]
View all windows with nth tag.
.TP
.B MOD\-Control\-[1..9]
Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B MOD\-Shift\-q
Quit dwm.
.TP
.B XF86AudioRaiseVolume / XF86AudioLowerVolume / XF86AudioMute
Adjust volume via
.BR pamixer (1).
.SS Mouse commands
.TP
.B MOD\-Button1
Move focused window while dragging. In a tiled layout, dragging onto another
tiled client swaps their positions instead of floating the window
(tiledmove patch); dragging a floating window moves it freely.
.TP
.B MOD\-Button2
Toggles focused window between floating and tiled state.
.TP
.B MOD\-Button3
Resize focused window while dragging; the pointer stays where you grabbed it
instead of warping to the window's corner (resizehere patch).
.SH STATUS TEXT FORMATTING
The status2d patch lets a status text generator (such as
.BR slstatus (1))
embed inline formatting codes in the root window name, delimited by
.BR ^ ... ^ :
.TP
.B ^c#RRGGBB^
Set the foreground (text) color.
.TP
.B ^b#RRGGBB^
Set the background color.
.TP
.B ^d^
Reset foreground/background to the default status color scheme.
.TP
.B ^r\fIx\fB,\fIy\fB,\fIw\fB,\fIh\fB^
Draw a filled rectangle at the given offset/size within the status area.
.TP
.B ^f\fIn\fB^
Advance the horizontal draw position by \fIn\fR pixels without drawing
anything.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwm is customized by editing config.def.h and (re)compiling the source code
(run
.B make && sudo make install
from the repository root). This keeps it fast, secure and simple. See
install.sh for a scripted Fedora setup, and README for the list of patches
this build carries on top of stock dwm.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR slstatus (1),
.BR kitty (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
This is a personal fork; file issues at the fedora-dwm repository rather than
upstream suckless.org unless you've reproduced them against vanilla dwm.
